### The three authorization shapes, reached in ONE system state so they are provably distinguishable
### rather than merely each reachable somewhere.
###
###   SHAPE 1  the operation has no rule at all          -> 403
###   SHAPE 2  a rule exists and the caller fails it     -> 200 with an empty page
###   SHAPE 3  a rule exists and ONE ROW fails it        -> 404
###
### Shape 2 is the one that has caught two briefs out: a rule compiles to a row-level predicate, so a
### caller who fails it is ALLOWED and simply matches no rows. It is not a gate.

### SHAPE 1. `regions` declares list, get and create — and no update and no delete. So there is no
### policy to consult, and the answer names the operation rather than the row.
# @name DeleteUnconfiguredOperation
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/regions/{{RegionCentralId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### The same shape on the other unconfigured verb, by the ADMIN key — so "the caller was too weak"
### cannot be the explanation. Nobody can update a region, because nothing says who may.
# @name AdminAlsoCannotUpdateRegion
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [403]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/regions/{{RegionCentralId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Renamed" }

### The control for shape 1: the SAME caller on the SAME entity, on a verb the descriptor configures.
### So the 403 above is the missing rule and not the entity or the key.
# @name SameCallerCanReadRegions
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/regions?limit=100&order=code.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### SHAPE 2. `customers.list` names dispatcher and admin. A technician holds neither, so the
### predicate is false for them — and the answer is 200 with nothing in it, not 403.
# @name TechnicianListsCustomers
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers?limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}

### The control for shape 2: the rows really are there, for a caller the predicate admits. Without
### this, an empty page would be indistinguishable from an empty table.
# @name DispatcherListsTheSameCustomers
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/customers?limit=100&order=name.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Shape 2 again with a ROW-LEVEL predicate rather than a caller-level one: `spare-north` is a
### technician with nothing assigned, so `assigned_to == @user.id` matches no row.
# @name SpareTechnicianListsWorkOrders
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keySpareNorth}}

### And the same predicate for a technician who IS assigned: a subset, not everything and not nothing
# @name AssignedTechnicianListsWorkOrders
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}

### SHAPE 3. WO-1004 exists and the technician is not assigned to it, so the row-level predicate
### excludes it — and an excluded row reads exactly like one that was never there.
# @name TechnicianReadsRowTheyAreNotAssigned
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1004Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}

### The control for shape 3: an id that genuinely does not exist answers the same 404, which is the
### indistinguishability the design promises.
# @name NobodyReadsANonexistentRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000ff
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}

### And the second control: the SAME technician reading a row they ARE assigned gets it. So the 404
### above is the predicate excluding a row and not the technician being unable to read at all.
# @name TechnicianReadsTheirOwnRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}

### A write is judged the same way: a technician may update a row assigned to them...
# @name TechnicianUpdatesTheirOwnRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "in_progress" }

### ...and not one they are not
# @name TechnicianUpdatesSomebodyElsesRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [404]
PATCH {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1004Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyTechNorth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "cancelled" }

### A credential that was presented and cannot be used is 401 — a different diagnosis from all three
### shapes above, and it owes a WWW-Authenticate challenge.
# @name UnusableCredential
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [401]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: dispatcher-north.not-the-secret

### The row the technician was refused is unchanged, read by a caller who can see it
# @name TheRefusedRowIsUnchanged
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1004Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}
